# Request cancellation

A request that a newer one can replace goes out with `AbortController`. That is search, filter, a table page, and leaving the screen. A new visit cancels the previous one. The response of a cancelled request is not written into state. `AbortError` does not become a journal error.

The signal is passed to `fetch`. Do not keep a custom `cancelled` flag or a second cancel timer next to the same request: cancellation is one, through the controller.

The controller lives with the caller of the read. A journal read accepts `signal` and knows nothing else about cancellation. The refresh timer also cancels its previous visit when the interval changed or the screen left. Two responses of one table are not both applied.

```tsx
const controller = new AbortController();
readObservations(controller.signal)
  .then(keep)
  .catch((error: unknown) => {
    if (error instanceof DOMException && error.name === "AbortError") return;
    setJournalError(true);
  });
return () => controller.abort();
```
